{"id": "civil_comments/test/20812", "source": "civil_comments", "primitive": "noul", "split": "test", "state": "\"Part 1\\nThat's not my experience, Don. In Byzantine churches, the \\\"throne\\\" is behind the altar and is literally a chair where the celebrant sits during the readings. If you trace this practice back in time, it was originally the \\\"apse\\\" with stadium-like seating for all of the clergy where they could see over top of the altar everything that was going on in the building. The \\\"inconstasis\\\" divides the church into \\\"sanctuary\\\" (behind) and \\\"nave\\\" (in front). \\nThis distinction of sanctuary and nave (behind and in front of the iconostasis) is of great importance in understanding data relevant to the ordination of woman as deacons--women deacons were ordained, along with male bishops, priests, and deacons, in the sanctuary, behind the inconostatis . All other clerics are ordained in the nave, in front of the iconostasis. This rubrical distinction puts the lie to those who insist that women deacons were never major clergy.\"", "question": "{\"type\": \"noul\", \"instructions\": \"Is this comment toxic?\", \"criteria\": {\"true\": \"Rude, disrespectful, or likely to make someone leave the discussion.\", \"false\": \"Civil, even if critical or blunt.\"}}", "label": "0", "soft_label": "0.0", "meta": "{\"hf_id\": \"google/civil_comments\", \"license\": \"cc0-1.0\", \"domain\": \"safety\", \"task_family\": \"toxicity\", \"hf_config\": \"default\", \"severe_toxicity\": 0.0}"}
